Abstract
Metal Organic Frameworks (MOFs) represent a promising class of metallic catalysts for reduction of nitrogen-containing contaminants (NCCs), such as 4-nitrophenol (4- NP). Nevertheless, most researches involving MOFs for 4-NP reduction employ noble metals in the form of fine powders, making these powdered noble metal-based MOFs impractical and inconvenient for realistic applications. Thus, it would be critical to develop non-noble-metal MOFs which can be incorporated into macroscale and porous supports for convenient applications. Herein, the present study proposes to develop a composite material which combines advantageous features of macroscale/porous supports, and nanoscale functionality of MOFs. In particular, copper foam (CF) is selected as a macroscale porous medium, which is covered by nanoflower-structured CoO to increase surfaces for growing a cobaltic MOF, ZIF-67. The resultant composite comprises of CF covered by CoO nanoflowers decorated with ZIF-67 to form a hierarchical 3D-structured catalyst, enabling this ZIF-67@Cu foam (ZIF@CF) a promising catalyst for reducing 4-NP, and other NCCs. Thus, ZIF@CF can readily reduce 4-NP to 4-AP with a significantly lower $E_{a}$ of $20\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ than reported values. ZIF@CF could be reused over 10 cycles and remain highly effective for 4-NP reduction. ZIF@CF also efficiently reduces other NCCs, such as 2-nitrophenol, 3-nitrophenol, methylene blue, and methyl orange. ZIF $@$ CF can be adopted as catalytic filters to enable filtration-type reduction of NCCs by passing NCC solutions through ZIF@CF to promptly and conveniently reduce NCCs. The versatile and advantageous catalytic activity of ZIF@CF validates that ZIF $\scriptstyle{\overbrace{(a)}}\mathrm{CF}$ is a promising and practical heterogeneous catalyst for reductive treatments of NCCs.
Keywords: MOFs, ZIF-67, nitrophenol, catalytic reduction, foam, cobalt
## 1. Introduction
Nitrogen-containing contaminants (NCCs) constitute a large fraction of pollutants in industrial wastewater [1]. Among various NCCs, nitrophenols (NPs), such as 4- nitrophenol (4-NP), gain extensive attention because discharge of 4-NP into natural water environment causes detrimental effects on aquatic ecology [2]. More importantly, 4-NP would also lead to negative effects on nerve and blood systems of human [3, 4], and induce methemoglobinemia [5]. Thus, it is necessary to remove 4-NP from wastewater, and prevent its discharge into the environment to avoid its adverse impact.
Nowadays, there have been several strategies introduced for removing 4-NP, including adsorption [6], chemical and photo-degradation [7], bio-degradation [8], as well as reductive treatments [9]. Among these strategies, reductive treatments of 4-NP are interesting and useful because reduction treatments would turn the nitro-group of 4- NP to an amine group, becoming 4-aminophenol (4-AP), which exhibits much less toxicity, but great benefit as 4-AP is a highly-valuable molecule for manufacturing pharmaceuticals and fine chemicals [10-13].
For reductive conversion of 4-NP to 4-AP, catalysts are required to react with reducing agents for hydrogenating the nitro group [3,6,7], and typically noble metals are necessitated to react with $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ as the reducing agent to reduce 4-NP to 4-AP [8– 12]. Nonetheless, costs of noble metals impede development and practical applications of using this catalytic process. Hence, it is crucial to design and establish non-noblemetal catalysts for 4-NP reduction.
Lately, a new category of metal-containing materials, Metal Organic Frameworks (MOFs), has been established and attracted great attention as MOFs can be designed to possess different transition metals with diversified structures [13]. Thus, several studies have proposed to use MOFs as catalysts for 4-NP reduction; however, most of reported studies of using MOFs for 4-NP reduction would still involve usage of noble metals (i.e., Ag, Au, and Pd) [14-18]. There are quite few researches reported for developing non-noble-metal MOFs to reduce 4-NP. Moreover, most of MOFs proposed for 4-NP reduction are in the form of fine particles, which are impractical and difficult to use in large-scale operations. Therefore, it would be more realistic to develop non-noble-metal MOFs which can be incorporated into large-sized, macroscale and even porous supports for convenient applications.
Thus, the present study proposes to develop an alternative composite material which can combine advantageous features of macroscale/porous supports, and nanoscale functionality of MOFs. In particular, copper (Cu) foam (CF) is selected because CF is an inexpensive and highly-accessible macroscale porous medium, which can be also easily functionalized, doped and modified with other components. As cobalt (Co) has been validated as an effective element for reducing 4-NP [19, 20], a Co-based MOF, zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF)-67, is specifically chosen to grow on CF. Nevertheless, since metal foams typically exhibit low surface areas for immobilizing/growing other components, CF in this study is firstly modified and covered by nanoflower-structured CoO to increase surfaces for growing ZIF-67. Such CoO nanoflowers not only act as supports for growing ZIF-67 but also serve as a source of Co for direct reaction with 2-imidazolate (2-MIM) to grow ZIF-67.
Thus, the resultant composite can comprise of the macroscale CF covered by nanoflower-structured CoO which is then decorated with ZIF-67 nanocrystals to form a hierarchical 3D-structured catalyst. Such a unique configuration enables this ZIF67@Cu foam (ZIF@CF) to exhibit macroscale merits of CF, and nanoscale reactivity to serve as a promising heterogeneous catalyst for reducing 4-NP, and other NCCs. As no such ZIF $@$ CF has been investigated for its catalytic activity in aqueous catalytic reduction of NCCs, and thus the present research offers useful information for fabricating such a composite, and essential findings and scientific insights into catalytic
characteristics of ZIF@CF.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Preparation of ZIF-67@Cu foam (ZIF@CF)
Fig. 1 illustrates the overall scheme of preparation of ZIF $\ @\mathrm{CF}$ . Firstly, CF (95 PPI, MayChun Company, Taiwan) was cut into a small piece (e.g., $3.5\mathrm{cm}{\times}1\mathrm{cm}{\times}0.1\mathrm{cm})$ , which was immersed in the concentrated HCl for $15\mathrm{min}$ , and rinsed thoroughly with DI water. Next, 2 mmol of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})$ (Union Chemicals, Taiwan), 8 mmol of $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ , 10 mmol of urea were dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water, followed by the addition of the pretreated CF. The resulting mixture was then heated at $120~^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the corresponding Co-modified CF was then rinsed with DI water, followed by a thermal treatment in $\Nu_{2}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ under $350~^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ to produce nanoflower-structured CoO@CF. Subsequently,  CoO@CF was immersed in a solution containing $5\mathrm{mL}$ of DI water, 5 mL of ethanol and 2 mmol of 2-Methylimidazole (2-MIM) (Acros Organics, USA) at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ to grow ZIF-67 on nanoflower-structured $\mathrm{CoO}@\mathrm{CF}$ , forming ZIF67@Cu foam (ZIF@CF).
The morphologies and element distribution of ZIF $@$ CF were examined by FESEM (JEOL JSM-7800F, Japan). XRD pattern of ZIF $@$ CF was investigated by an Xray diffractometer (Bruker D8 Discover, USA). FT-IR spectrum of ZIF@CF was analyzed by FTIR (Jasco 6700, USA). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was also employed to analyze surface chemistry of ZIF@CF (PHI 5000, ULVAC-PHI, Japan). Textural properties of ZIF@CF were determined using a volumetric gas adsorption analyzer (Anton Paar Novatouch LX2, Austria) for obtaining $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms.
2.2 Catalytic reduction of nitrogen-containing contaminants (NCCs) by ZIF@CF In a typical experiment of catalytic reduction of NCCs, a NCC (e.g., 4-NP) aqueous solution with an initial concentration of $3.6\times10^{-3}\mathrm{M}$ was prepared, and a 1 M of $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ aqueous solution was also obtained. Next, $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ of aforementioned 4-NP aqueous solution was introduced and then $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ of 1M of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ aqueous solution was added to a quartz cuvette containing $2.5\mathrm{mL}$ DI water. Subsequently, a small piece of ZIF@CF $(3~\mathrm{mg})$ ) was introduced into the cuvette. After pre-set intervals, UV-vis spectra of the mixture were recorded over the scanning region of $200{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ . The determination of 4-NP concentration was performed by measuring the absorbance after different intervals at $400\mathrm{nm}$ . Furthermore, catalytic reduction experiments of other NCCs (e.g., 2-nitrophenol (2-NP), 3-nitrophenol (3-NP), methylene blue (MB), and methyl orange (MO)) were implemented through the similar procedure as for 4-NP reduction.
For the filtration-type reaction, as the size of ZIF@CF used in this type of test was relatively thin $(0.1\ \mathrm{cm})$ , the injection rate was relatively slow (ca. $2\ \mathrm{ml/min}$ ) and controlled manually in order to increase the contact time for NCC, $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ and ZIF $@$ CF for reducing NCCs.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of ZIF@CF
As CF was employed as the template to grow CoO and ZIF-67, the appearance of CF was first visualized in Fig. 2(a), and the pristine CF exhibited the typical reddish brown color. Furthermore, the microscopic image of CF can be seen in Fig. 2(b), in which the skeletons of the foamy structure can be easily observed, and the surface of the skeleton (Fig. 2(c)) was very smooth without noticeable additional objects. When CF was modified by Co species through the hydrothermal process and then the thermal treatment, the skeletons of CF were fully covered by the flower-like structures (Fig. 2(d-f)) which seemed assembles of inter-stacked and inter-penetrated nanosheets with a thickness of $150{\sim}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The corresponding XRD pattern of this nanoflowercovered CF was displayed in Fig. 4(a). When the pristine CF exhibited two signature peaks at 43.2 and $50.4^{\circ}$ , attributed to the (111) and (200) planes of Cu, respectively [21, 22], the XRD pattern of this nanoflower-covered CF showed an additional peak at $37^{\circ}$ , which corresponded to the (111) diffraction plane of CoO [23], validating that these nanoflower structures were comprised of Co in the form of CoO.
After CoO nanoflower-covered CF then further reacted with 2-MIM to convert CoO into ZIF-67, the resulting product can be seen in Fig. 2(g), in which the nanoflower structures can be still retained. However, the closer view (Fig. 2(h)) shows that surfaces of sheets in the nanoflower structure became noticeably roughened and decorated with very fine components. An even closer view (Fig. 2(i)) further revealed that these fine components exhibited a well-defined dodecahedral morphology, which is the typical shape of ZIF-67 [24-28], suggesting that ZIF-67 was successfully grown onto the sheets of nanoflower structures at CF. The fraction of ZIF-67 in the entire ZIF@CF was quantified gravimetrically by weight changes from a piece of CF to ${\mathrm{CoO}}\ @{\mathrm{CF}}$ , and then ZIF@CF, indicating that the fraction of ZIF-67 was $5.5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of ZIF@CF.
Fig. 4(a) further displays the XRD pattern of the resultant product, which had a series of additional peaks in the range of 5 to $30^{\circ}$ , while the peak of CoO at $37^{\circ}$ and the peaks of Cu at 43.2 and $50.4^{\circ}$ can be still observed. These additional peaks at $7.2^{\circ}$ , 10.2°, $12.6^{\circ}$ , $13.9^{\circ}$ , $16.0^{\circ}$ , $18.5^{\circ}$ , $14.5^{\circ}$ , and $29.8^{\circ}$ , which can be well-indexed to the (011), (002), (112), (022), (013),(222), (233), and (044) planes of ZIF-67 [28-30], validating these dodecahedral fine components grown on the nanoflower structures were ZIF-67 nanocrystals, and a part of CoO was converted into ZIF-67.
The picture of this ZIF@CF can be also seen in Fig. 2(a), showing that the original reddish brown CF had been completely transformed into the dark purple foam. The large-field elemental mapping result of this ZIF@CF (Fig. 2(j-l)) further indicates that the skeletons of ZIF@CF were fully covered by Co species as Cu was barely detected. Fig. 3 also displays the elemental mapping analysis on the sheet of nanoflower at CF. As the original pristine CoO and Cu did not contain carbon and nitrogen, the well-distributed signals of C and N (derived from 2-MIM) validated that the sheet was evenly and fully covered by ZIF-67.
ZIF@CF was then further analyzed by FTIR to elucidate the chemical variation on the surface as shown in Fig 4 (b). In the case of pristine CF, the broad peak appeared at $3440~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is ascribed to the stretching vibration absorption of –OH groups at CF surfaces. In comparison to the IR spectrum of pristine CF [21], a number of additional peaks can be found in ZIF@CF. In particular, the peaks at 562, 660, $754\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ could be attributed to Co-O group, and the peak at $1411\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was derived from the stretching of imidazole ring [31], also validating that ZIF-67 was successfully grown on the surface of CF. Furthermore, as the skeleton surfaces of CF had been changed substantially, it was interesting to probe into its variation of textural properties. Thus, $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms of the pristine of CF and ZIF@CF were both measured and shown in Fig. 4(c). While the amount of $\Nu_{2}$ sorption in CF was very low, the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption was considerably increased before of the existence of ZIF-67. As the surface area of CF was merely $20\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ ZIF $@$ CF can exhibit a substantially higher surface area of $50\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . On the other hand, the pore size distribution of ZIF $\mathsf{i}@\mathrm{CF}$ revealed that much more micropores and mesopores were detected in ZIF@CF in comparison to the pristine CF. As a result, ZIF $\ @\mathrm{CF}$ can exhibit a much higher total pore volume of $0.025\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ than CF $(0.017~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ , validating that the presence of ZIF-67 considerably modified the textural feature of CF and enhanced it surficial porosity.
## 3.2 Catalytic reduction of 4-NP by ZIF@CF
Before investigating catalytic activities of ZIF $@$ CF for reduction of 4-NP, it would be necessary to examine whether 4-NP would be eliminated by ZIF@CF via adsorption in the absence of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Fig. S1(a) shows UV spectrum of 4-NP in the presence of ZIF@CF, and no significant variation in the spectrum can be observed over $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ , indicating that 4-NP would not be removed through adsorption to ZIF@CF.
As ZIF@CF was derived from CF and CoO@CF, it would be critical to examine if CF and CoO@CF would catalyze 4-NP reduction. Thus, NaBH4 was then added to the 4-NP solution, and the color of 4-NP solution was changed from light yellow to bright yellow because of the deprotonation of 4-NP [32] with the spectral shift from $316\mathrm{nm}$ to $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. S1(b) displays that the spectrum of 4-NP remained almost the same in the presence of CF and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ after $120~\mathrm{min}$ , demonstrating that CF itself cannot catalyze reduction of 4-NP. Next, when $\mathrm{CoO}@\mathrm{CF}$ and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ were added to a 4-NP solution, the spectrum of 4-NP started to change and the peak at $400\mathrm{nm}$ gradually decreased and fully disappeared after $70\mathrm{min}$ with the occurrence of the peak of 4-AP at $300\mathrm{nm}$ . This result indicates that CoO@CF could catalyze 4-NP reduction; however the reduction rate seemed quite slow.
On the other hand, when ZIF@CF and NaBH4 were added to a 4-NP solution, the peak at $400\mathrm{nm}$ decreased rapidly and fully removed within 15 min with the occurrence of the peak of 4-AP at $300\mathrm{nm}$ . The inset in Fig. 5(a) also shows that the solution of 4- NP was fully decolorized by ZIF@CF, validating the successful conversion of 4-NP to 4-AP which was colorless. This result shows that ZIF@CF could also catalyze reduction of 4-NP and the catalytic activity of ZIF@CF seemed much higher than CoO@CF in view of degradation rate.
To further quantify the degradation kinetics, the pseudo first-order reaction was adopted as follows [33-35]:
$$
L n\left(C_{t}/C_{0}\right)=-k t
$$
Where $C_{t}$ is the concentration of 4-NP and $C_{\theta}$ is the initial concentration of 4-NP, $t$ is reaction time and $k$ is the first-order rate constant of 4-NP reduction. When k for the 4- NP reduction by $\mathrm{CoO}@\mathrm{CF}$ was $0.0618\ \mathrm{\min^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , ZIF@CF could exhibit a significantly higher $\mathbf{k}$ as $0.2946\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , validating that $Z_{\mathrm{IF}@{\mathrm{CF}}}$ possessed a higher catalytic activity, and the decoration of ZIF onto CoO@CF considerably improved its catalytic activity. While the rate constant of 4-NP reduction by ZIF-67 particle was determined as $0.3843\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ (Fig. S2), validating that ZIF-67 is certainly an advantageous catalyst for 4-NP reduction, direct addition of ZIF-67 particle into aqueous solutions would certainly cause severe issues, such as difficult recovery and secondary pollution. Nevertheless, as ZIF-67 was grown directly on CF, the textural properties of the pristine Cu foam can be considerably enhanced by growing ZIF-67 to increase the surface area for boosting up the reactive contact surface through the multilayered structure of ZIF $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{O}}\mathrm{CF}$ . More importantly, these ZIF-67 particles on CoO nanoflower supported on Cu foam can also maintain its catalytic activity for reducing NCCs in the presence of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Thus, the significantly higher catalytic activity of ZIF@CF over CoO@CF and CF was attributed to the successful development of such a hierarchically-structured composite on which ZIF-67 can be grown on the more contact surface for enhancing its reaction with 4-NP.
Additionally, as activation energy $(E_{a})$ is an important index for evaluating catalytic activity of ZIF-67 foam for the reduction of 4-NP, rate constants of 4-NP reduction at different temperatures were then obtained to draw the Arrhenius plot of $L n$ $k$ vs $l/T$ as shown in Fig. S3 (see the supporting information) according to $L n k=L n$ $A-E_{a}/R T.$ The as-calculated $E_{a}$ value was $20.0\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , which was further compared with other reported $E_{a}$ values as listed in Table 1, showing that ZI $\mathrm{}^{\overrightarrow{+}}\textcircled{a}\mathrm{CF}$ exhibited a significantly lower $E_{a}$ value than most of reported catalysts, including noble metal catalysts. This validates that $Z_{\mathrm{IF}@{\mathrm{CF}}}$ was certainly an advantageous and promising catalyst for 4-NP reduction.
Table 1. Comparison of rate constant $(k)$ , and $E_{a}$ of ZIF-67 foam and different reported materials for the reduction of 4-NP.
Moreover, as ZIF $@$ CF was proposed as a convenient heterogeneous catalyst, its recyclability was also essential and thus evaluated in Fig. 5(b). When ZIF $\ @\mathrm{CF}$ was continuously adopted for 4-NP reduction without any regeneration treatments, ZIF@CF can exhibit a stable and effective catalytic activity for achieving full reduction of 4-NP over 10 cycles. The rate constants over 10 cycles also remained similar without significant decreases. The morphology of the used ZIF@CF had been also characterized (Fig. S4) and it was similar to that of the pristine one, and the shape of ZIF-67 particles can be still retained without significant changes. The XRD pattern of the used ZIF@CF was also further analyzed (Fig. S5), and it was very comparable to that of the pristine ZIF@CF without significant changes. These features validated that ZIF@CF was a durable and usable catalyst.
To further examine the mechanism of 4-NP reduction by $Z_{\mathrm{IF}@{\mathrm{CF}}}$ , variation of surface chemistry before and after 4-NP reduction was determined. Fig. 5(c) shows the core-level Co2p spectrum of the pristine ZIF@CF, which can reveal underlying 4 peaks after deconvolution. In particular, the peaks at $781.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $796.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to $C0^{2+}$ , whereas the other adjacent peaks were satellite peaks [43]. Nevertheless, the Co2p spectrum of the spent ZIF $\scriptstyle{\overbrace{(a)}}\mathrm{CF}$ was noticeably different from that of the pristine ZIF $\ @\mathrm{CF}$ as six underlying peaks can be obtained after deconvolution. In addition to the peaks of $C0^{2+}$ , and satellite peaks, two significant peaks at $780.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $795.6\mathrm{eV}$ can be detected and ascribed to $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ [44]. The presence of $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ suggested that $C0^{2+}$ of ZIF $@$ CF might denote an electron for reducing 4-NP to become ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ , which would be transformed back to $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ by the reducing agent, $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Thus, a possible reaction pathway of 4-NP by ZIF@CF can be then illustrated in Fig. 6. At first, 4-NP would be adsorbed to ZIF $\ @\mathrm{CF}$ and its nitro-functional group would then gain an electron from $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ of $Z\mathrm{IF}@C\mathrm{F}$ , forming 4-AP. Next, 4-AP would be desorbed from Z $\mathrm{IF}@\mathrm{CF}$ to the bulk solution. Simultaneously, $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}^{-}$ has been validated to reduce $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ back to $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ in aqueous solutions [45-47] . As a result, $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ can be both detected on the surface of ZIF@CF.
3.3 Catalytic performance of ZIF $@$ CF for other NCCs.
As ZIF@CF was successfully demonstrated to reduce 4-NP, it was critical to evaluate whether ZIF@CF can also reduce other NCCs. Firstly, 2-NP and 3-NP, which are analogues to 4-NP, were tested, and their spectral variations are displayed in Fig. 7(ab). As 2-NP could not be eliminated through adsorption as shown in Fig. S4, the signature peak of 2-NP at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ could be quickly decreased and almost fully disappeared within $40~\mathrm{min}$ in the presence of ZIF $\ @\mathrm{CF}$ and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . The inset of Fig. 7(a) also validates that the yellowish color of 2-NP solution was also fully decolorized, conforming that ZIF ${\mathrm{:}}\ @\operatorname{CF}$ can also reduce 2-NP. The corresponding $k$ of 2-NP reduction by ZIF@CF was determined as $0.058\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , which was much faster than many reported $k$ values by other catalysts (Table S1), validating that ZIF@CF exhibited an advantageous catalytic activity for reduction of 2-NP.
Similarly, ZIF@CF can also successfully and efficiently reduce 3-NP within 16 min as validated in the inset of Fig. 7(b). The corresponding $k$ value was also faster than many reported $k$ values by other catalysts (Table S2). These results ascertained that ZIF $@$ CF can be applied for reducing different analogues of nitrophenols.
In addition to nitrophenols, many dye molecules are also NCCs and could be decolorized through reductive reactions by hydrogenating its nitrogen group of phenothiazine [48-50]. Thus, ZIF $@$ CF was then also tested for these nitro-aromatic dyes, including MB and MO. As MB could not be decolorized by Z $\mathrm{IF}@\mathrm{CF}$ via adsorption (Fig. S6) either, Fig. 7(c) shows spectral variation of MB in the presence of ZIF@CF and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ , in which the signature peaks of MB were rapidly decreased and almost disappeared within $4~\mathrm{min}$ . The inset of Fig. 7(a) also validates that the color of MB was completely decolorized. The corresponding $k$ was determined as $0.917\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , which was noticeably higher than many reported $k$ values by various catalysts (Table S3). In the case of MO, the signature peaks of MO were also rapidly decreased and completely vanished in 5 min. The inset of Fig. 7(b) also validates that the color of MO was fully decolorized and became colorless. The corresponding $k$ appeared substantially higher than reported $k$ values in literatures (Table S4). These results verified that ZIF@CF exhibited versatile and promising catalytic activities towards reduction of NCCs.
The recyclability of ZIF@CF for reduction of 2-nitrophenol (2-NP), 3-nitrophenol (3-NP), methylene blue (MB), and methyl orange (MO) was also evaluated as displayed in Fig. S7. In the case of 2-NP as Fig. Fig. S7(a), the rate constant slightly decreased from 0.058 to $0.0572\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , and the conversion efficiency still exhibited high as 98.2 $\%$ . Besides, Fig. S7(b) shows the reduction of 3-NP over 10 continuous cycles, the conversion efficiency maintained a comparable as $98\%$ after 10 cycles, and the rate constant as $0.1877~\mathrm{{min^{-1}}}$ also similar to first cycle $(0.1880\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ ). Moreover, the reduction of MB and MO were conducted over 10 cycles as exhibited in Fig. S7(c-d), the rate constant of MB exhibits stable $0.917\mathrm{~min^{-1}}$ for the fresh and $0.916\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ after 10 cycles, on the other hand, Fig. S7(d) exhibits the rate constant of MO as $0.7310\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ for the fresh and $0.727\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ after 10 cycles. The corresponding conversion efficiency also shows an excellent performance as $98\%$ after 10 cycles. While Z $\mathrm{IF@CF}$ was continuously conducted for reduction of NCCs without any regeneration treatment, ZIF67@CF still exhibit a comparable catalytic activity and a stable ability for eliminating NCCs over 10 cycles.
## 3.4 Filtration-type reduction of NCCs by ZIF@CF
Aside from the batch-type demonstration of using ZIF@CF for reducing various NCCs, the mesh-structured feature would enable ZIF@CF to be used for filtration-type reductive reactions. Fig. 8(a) shows a syringe containing a yellowish solution of 4-NP together with $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . Once the syringe was pressed to flow the yellowish 4-NP solution through ZIF@CF, the yellowish solution promptly became colorless. The filtrate was also examined and the signature peak of 4-NP (at $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) had vanished, but the occurrence of 4-AP was successfully detected.
On the other hand, Fig. 8(b) demonstrates that MB could be also immediately decolorized by filtrating a solution of MB and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ through ZIF@CF. The spectral variation confirmed that the signature peaks of MB at 660 and 590 nm both disappeared after the filtration. Similar results can be observed in the case of MO (Fig. 8(c)), and the orange color was quickly decolorized when the solution of MO and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ was passed through ZIF $@$ CF with disappearance of a signature peak at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ . These results ascertained that ZIF@CF can be conveniently, and easily as filters for reducing and decolorizing NCCs promptly.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, a facile technique was reported to fabricate ZIF@CF as a hierachicallystructured 3D catalyst, comprised of a marcoscale porous CF which was fully covered by CoO nanoflowers decorated with ZIF-67 nanocrystals. Such a hierachical and multicomponent configuration, providing large surfaces for growing and immobilizing ZIF67, forming a versatile, convenient and efficient heterogenous catalyst for reductive treatment of NCCs. Thus, Z $\mathrm{IF}@\mathrm{CF}$ can readily reduce 4-NP to 4-AP with a significantly lower $E_{a}$ of $20.0\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ than many other reported $E_{a}$ values, including several noble metal catalysts, validating its advantageous catalytic activity. In addition, ZIF $@$ CF could be reused over 10 cycles and remained highly effective and durable for 4-NP reduction. Via XPS investigation, the mechanism of catalytic 4-NP reduction by ZIF $@$ CF was also elucidated and ascribed to the redox transformation of Co species of ZIF@CF by $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . ZIF@CF was also confirmed to efficiently reduce and decolorize other nitrophenols, such as 2-NP and 3-NP, and other nitro-containing dyes, MB, and MO. More importantly, ZIF@CF can be also adopted as catalytic filters to enable filtration-type reduction of NCCs by passing NCC solutions through ZIF@CF to promptly and conveniently reduce NCCs. The versatile and advantageous catalytic activity of ZIF@CF validates that ZIF $@$ CF is a promising and practical heterogeneous catalyst for reductive treatments of NCCs. The fabrication approach for ZIF@CF reported here also provides useful techniques for designing, developing and creating such a hierarchical, and versatile heterogeneous catalyst.
